# TicketScout — Cloudflare Pages redirects
#
# ROUTING ARCHITECTURE:
# - /concert/[slug]  → static file at concert/[slug].html  (no redirect needed)
# - /football/[slug] → static file at football/[slug].html (no redirect needed)
# - /theatre/[slug]  → static file at theatre/[slug].html  (no redirect needed)
# - /venue/[slug]    → static file at venue/[slug].html    (no redirect needed)
#
# football/index.html, theatre/index.html, concert/index.html now handle
# /football/, /theatre/, /concert/ landing pages directly.
# The old /football → /football.html rules are REMOVED because they caused
# ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS when stubs tried to fetch /football.html
#
# CRITICAL: Do NOT add catch-all redirects for category subpaths
# Do NOT add /football → /football.html (causes redirect loop with football/ directory)

/concerts  /concert.html   200


# ===========================================================================
# SITEMAP INDEX — added 9 Aug 2026
# ===========================================================================
#
# WHY: robots.txt points Google at /sitemap.xml, which is a STATIC file
# listing the seven child sitemaps with no <lastmod> at all. <lastmod> on a
# sitemap index is Google's primary signal for which child sitemaps to
# re-fetch and how often — on a registry that auto-commits pages
# continuously, without it Google has no way to know the concert section
# changed today. On a ticket site, speed-to-index is the business.
#
# A static file fundamentally cannot carry an accurate <lastmod>: any date
# committed into it is stale the moment the next entity auto-commits, and
# Google disregards <lastmod> SITEWIDE once it decides the values are
# untrustworthy — strictly worse than emitting none at all.
#
# /api/sitemap?sec=index already generates a correct index with real per-
# section dates (see functions/api/sitemap.js — each date is derived from the
# same data the child sitemaps emit per URL, never Date.now()).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRECEDENCE: in Cloudflare Pages a STATIC FILE at a path ALWAYS wins over
# any _redirects rule or Function at that same path. So while /sitemap.xml
# exists as a real file in the repo root, the /sitemap.xml rule below is
# INERT. That is deliberate — see the staged rollout.
#
# This is also why an earlier attempt using a Pages Function
# (functions/sitemap.xml.js) appeared to do nothing, then 404'd once the
# static file was deleted. NEVER delete the static file until the replacement
# is PROVEN to serve correctly.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STAGED ROLLOUT — in order, do not skip step 2:
#
#   1. Deploy this file with the static /sitemap.xml STILL IN PLACE.
#      Nothing changes. Zero risk.
#
#   2. Visit https://ticketscout.co.uk/sitemap-test.xml
#      - XML with <lastmod> on each <sitemap> entry → mechanism works,
#        go to step 3.
#      - 404 page → the rewrite does not reach Function routes on this
#        deployment. STOP. Do not touch /sitemap.xml. Use Plan B below.
#
#      (Why this test is necessary even though /concerts above proves 200
#      rewrites work: that rule rewrites to a STATIC file. These rewrite to a
#      FUNCTION route carrying a query string — similar, but not the same
#      code path. Prove it rather than assume it.)
#
#   3. Only after step 2 passes: delete the static /sitemap.xml from the repo
#      root and redeploy. /sitemap.xml then serves the dynamic index.
#
#   4. Verify /sitemap.xml shows <lastmod>, and that the values are VARIED
#      across sections rather than all identical to today — all-identical-to-
#      today is the exact pattern that makes Google distrust lastmod sitewide.
#
#   5. Once /sitemap.xml is confirmed, the sitemap-test.xml rule can be
#      deleted. Harmless to leave, but it is scaffolding, not a feature.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PLAN B — if step 2 fails, needs no routing behaviour at all:
#
#   Leave the static /sitemap.xml where it is, delete the two rules below,
#   and change ONE line in robots.txt:
#       Sitemap: https://ticketscout.co.uk/api/sitemap?sec=index
#   Then submit that same URL in GSC > Sitemaps.
#
#   Google accepts a sitemap at any URL — it need not be named sitemap.xml
#   nor sit at the root. robots.txt already has "Allow: /api/sitemap" in
#   every crawler group, so it is fetchable. Durable, and cannot 404.
# ===========================================================================

# Step 2 test rule (scaffolding — delete once /sitemap.xml is confirmed)
/sitemap-test.xml  /api/sitemap?sec=index  200

# The real rule. INERT until the static /sitemap.xml is deleted (step 3).
/sitemap.xml  /api/sitemap?sec=index  200
